
{"id":"6ffceb94-4321-4aa5-a110-dd09b547f3c8","object":"chat.completion","created":1784817372,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_of = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError('duplicate id')\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children_of.setdefault(pid, []).append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            root = nid\\n    # validate structure\\n    if 'root' not in node_map:\\n        raise ValueError('missing root')\\n    if node_map['root']['parent'] is not None:\\n        raise ValueError('root has parent')\\n    # ensure all required symbols present\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    for req in required_symbols:\\n        if symbol_counts.get(req, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError('required symbol missing')\\n    # initial kept children count\\n    kept_child_count = {}\\n    for nid in node_map:\\n        kept_child_count[nid] = len(children_of.get(nid, []))\\n    # processing order: descending depth, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed_set:\\n            continue\\n        # check mandatory keep conditions\\n        if node['parent'] is None:  # root\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if kept_child_count[nid] > 0:\\n            continue\\n        # check required symbols coverage after removal\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue  # would lose last occurrence\\n        # remove this node\\n        removed_set.add(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        # update parent's kept child count\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            kept_child_count[pid] -= 1\\n    # collect retained ids and sort\\n    retained = [nid for nid in node_map if nid not in removed_set]\\n    retained.sort()\\n    return {'retained': retained, 'removed': removed_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We are asked to implement a function `solve(case)` that performs a \"grammar-guided node reduction\" on a tree of nodes. The case payload includes a list of nodes (each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), and required_symbols. The task is to reduce the tree by tentatively removing removable nodes (with certain constraints) while preserving coverage of required symbols and some \"interestingness oracle\" (which seems to be implicitly defined by the utility? Actually the description mentions \"interestingness oracle\" but no explicit predicate. However from the context of Perses-style reduction, we likely need to remove nodes when possible, but we must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., there is some node with that symbol remaining). Since we have a tree structure, removing a node also removes its descendants. The constraints: root is not removable. Required symbols must remain represented. Also we cannot remove a node that has retained children (i.e., if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree; but if we keep some children, that implies the node itself is kept? Actually the typical Perses reduction: we can delete a node (remove it and its subtree) or replace it with one of its children (i.e., replace the node with a child, thus removing the node but keeping that child and its subtree). But the description here only mentions \"delete and replacement candidates\", but the typical Perses uses both deletion and replacement (e.g., replacing a node with one of its children). However the fixture data does not have any \"replacements\" defined. The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And the output should be sorted retained IDs and actual removal order. So likely it's just deletion: we can mark nodes as removed (or keep them). But there is also \"grammar_valid\" – we must ensure that after reduction, the resulting tree is still \"grammar valid\" according to some grammar? The task mentions \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" but there is no grammar provided. Possibly the grammar is implicitly defined by the required_symbols? Or the tree itself is already grammar-valid? The description says \"parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded\" so we don't have a grammar. Perhaps the only constraint is that required_symbols must be present, and we cannot remove the root or required symbol nodes, and we cannot remove nodes that have retained children (i.e., if we keep a child, we must keep the parent because the parent is needed to connect the child? But the tree is given as a list of nodes with parent references. If we remove a node, we also remove its children. So we cannot remove a node if we intend to keep any of its descendants. So the decision is simply: which removable nodes can be removed such that all required symbols are still present somewhere (i.e., there is at least one node with that symbol that is not removed). Also we must consider depth order: \"deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\". So we should process nodes in decreasing depth (deepest first). At equal depth, use node ID as stable tie break. For each node (if removable and not root and not required symbol?), we tentatively consider removing it. But we need to ensure required symbols remain. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The last part: \"a node with retained children\" – that means if we decide to keep any of its children, we cannot remove the parent. Since we process deepest first, children will be considered before parents. If a child is kept (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed. So we need to track which nodes are kept/removed. Initially all nodes are kept. When we consider a node, we check if we can remove it: it must be removable, not root, not required symbol (the required_symbols list contains symbols; we should not remove nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols? Actually the description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we should not remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. Also we cannot remove a node if it has any child that is not removed (i.e., retained). Since we process deep first, the children status is already decided. So we need to check: if the node is removable and not root and its symbol is not in required_symbols, and none of its children (in the original tree) are kept (i.e., all its descendants have been removed? Actually \"retained children\" means children that are kept. Since we process deep first, we will have already decided for all descendants. But careful: \"node with retained children\" – if any child is retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent because removing the parent would also remove that child. But if all children are removed, then removing the parent is okay. However note that children removal might be due to us deciding to remove them. So we can remove a node if all its children are also removed. But also we must check that after removing this node, the required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree. Since we are removing a node and its entire subtree, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node (outside the removed subtree) that has that symbol. Note that required symbols may be satisfied by nodes that are ancestors or in other branches. After removal, the tree structure changes? Actually removal just marks node as removed; but we must also consider that the parent-child relationships are broken. However for the purpose of determining if required symbols remain, we consider all nodes that are not removed. So we can compute before removal the set of symbols present among all nodes that are kept. Then when tentatively removing a node, we check if after removal, the required symbols would still be present. Because removal removes the node and its descendants (if any). So we need to know the subtree rooted at that node. We have parent pointers, we can build a children mapping. Then processing deepest first: nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending. For each node, if it is eligible for removal (removable, not root, symbol not in required_symbols), we check if all its descendants (including itself) that are still kept? Actually we need to check children: if any child is kept (not removed), then we cannot remove the node. So we must have already processed children, and we know if they are removed or not. So condition: node can be removed if no child is kept (i.e., all children are removed). Then we also need to check required symbols: after removing this node, will all required symbols still be present? Since we will remove the node and its entire subtree, we must ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node outside this subtree that has that symbol. That includes ancestors (but ancestors are above) and other branches. Note that required symbols might be satisfied by the node itself, but if we remove it, we need another node. So we can compute a count of nodes per required symbol among all kept nodes (including this node's subtree). Then if after removal, the count for any required symbol becomes zero, we cannot remove. Since we process in order, the counts need to be updated as we remove nodes. So we need to maintain a map from symbol to count of kept nodes that have that symbol, and also a set of removed node IDs. Initially all nodes are kept. We precompute symbol counts. Then for each node in order, we evaluate removal conditions. If conditions satisfied, we mark it as removed (add to removed set) and decrement counts for its symbol and for all descendants' symbols (because they are removed as well). But careful: since we process deep first, descendants have already been considered. If a descendant was removed earlier, its symbol counts have already been decremented. So when we remove a parent, we should only decrement counts for nodes that are actually still kept in its subtree. That is, we need to know which descendants are still kept. Since we process deep first, when we encounter a node, all its descendants have already been processed, and some might have been removed. So the subtree of a node may have some nodes already removed. When we remove the node, we are effectively also removing any remaining kept nodes in its subtree (its children that were kept, and their kept descendants, etc.). So we need to recursively remove all kept nodes in the subtree. But simpler: we can maintain a \"removed\" flag and when we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree (including already removed ones? but they are already removed). For counting, we need to decrement counts for all nodes that are being removed that were previously kept. Since we process deep first, the children that were kept have not been removed, so they are still in the count. So we can, when removing a node, remove it and all its kept descendants (those that are not already removed). We can traverse the subtree and decrement counts for each node that is not already removed, and mark them removed. But we must be careful about order: we are processing nodes in decreasing depth, so when we consider a parent, its children are shallower? No: parent depth is less than children depth. So we process children first (deeper) then parent. So for a parent, its children have already been processed. If a child was removable and we decided to remove it, it is already removed. If a child was not removable or we decided to keep it, it is still kept. So when we later consider the parent, we check if any child is kept. If all children are removed, we may remove the parent. When we remove the parent, we also remove all kept grandchildren? But grandchildren are already processed? Actually process order: depth descending, so grandchildren (depth 3) are processed before children (depth 2) which are processed before parent (depth 1). So when we process a child, we have already processed its own children (grandchildren of parent). So if a child was kept, its own children might have been removed or kept. But that is fine because when we remove the parent, we also remove the child and its subtree, which includes all still-kept nodes under that child. Since the child is kept, it hasn't been removed yet. So we need to remove the child and its entire kept subtree. So we need to traverse the subtree of the parent and remove all kept nodes. That could be done recursively. But we also need to update the counts accordingly. Since we are processing in order, we can do: for node in sorted order (deep to shallow): if node is eligible (removable, not root, not required symbol, and no kept children?), then we compute the effect of removing the entire subtree rooted at that node (including node itself) from the current state. But we must be careful: if some descendants have already been removed, they are not in the kept set, so we don't double-count. We can compute the set of nodes that would be removed if we remove this node: it is node itself plus any kept descendants (since those are still kept). We can perform a DFS from node, only traversing through children that are still kept. For each such node, we check if it is already removed? Not possible because we only remove nodes through this process, and we process deep first. But if we are removing a node, its descendants that are kept haven't been removed yet (since if they were removable, they would have been processed earlier and possibly removed, but if they are kept, they weren't removed). So we can assume none of the kept descendants have been removed. However there is a subtlety: a descendant might be \"removable\" but we decided not to remove it because it was required or had kept children etc. So it is still kept. So yes, we need to remove the entire kept subtree.\n\nBut the description also says \"Never remove a node with retained children.\" That implies we only consider removing a node if none of its children are retained. So that condition is a prerequisite. So when we evaluate a node, we check if any of its children (direct children) are still kept. If any child is kept, we cannot remove the node. So we skip. Only if all children are removed (or node has no children) can we consider removal. Then we also check required symbols. Then if both conditions satisfied, we remove the node (and its subtree, but since all children are already removed, the only nodes in the subtree that are kept are the node itself? Wait: if all children are removed, that means the node's direct children have been removed. But what about grandchildren? If a child is removed, its entire subtree was removed when that child was processed. So if all direct children are removed, then the entire subtree below the node is already removed. So the only node that remains in the subtree is the node itself. So actually, if we have processed deep first, and we only remove nodes when all their children are removed, then by the time we consider a parent, its children have been processed: if a child was removed, its subtree is gone. If a child was kept, that child is still there. So condition \"no retained children\" means that all children are removed. If all children are removed, then the subtree of the node currently consists only of the node itself (since all descendants were removed when their parents were removed). So in that case, removing the node is just removing that single node. That simplifies things: we don't need to remove a whole subtree, just the node. The counts: only the node's symbol count needs to be decremented. So we can maintain a map from symbol to count of kept nodes. Initially count all nodes. Then for each node in order (depth descending, ID ascending), if it is removable, not root, symbol not in required_symbols, and all its children are removed (i.e., none of its children are kept), then we check if after removing this node, the required symbols would still have at least one kept node (i.e., for each required symbol, its count after removal would be >0). If yes, we mark it as removed: remove node from kept set, decrement count for its symbol. Also we might need to update the \"children\" status for its parent? The parent will later check if its children are kept. When we remove a node, its parent's list of children becomes one less kept child. So we need to propagate that information. Since we process from deep to shallow, the parent hasn't been processed yet. So we can maintain for each node a count of kept children, or a set. Initially each node's kept children count = number of children. When we remove a node, we decrease its parent's kept children count by 1. So when we later evaluate the parent, we can check if kept children count == 0. That works.\n\nAlso we need to consider that the root is never removable. Required symbol nodes are not removable (their symbol appears in required_symbols list). Also nodes that are not removable (removable == false) cannot be removed.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Build nodes dict by id. Build children list for each node.\n2. Identify root (parent null). Ensure exactly one root.\n3. Build set of required_symbols.\n4. Compute initial count of nodes per symbol: all nodes contribute.\n5. Initialize for each node: kept_children_count = number of children (direct children). Also we track a set of kept nodes? Actually we track removal status: initially all nodes are kept = True. But we only need to know kept children count for parent. When a node is removed, we update its parent's kept_children_count.\n6. Create list of node IDs sorted by depth descending, then ID ascending (as string? IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", but ordering might be lexicographic? We'll use natural ordering of the strings as provided. The description says \"use node ID as the stable tie break\". Since IDs are strings, we can sort by depth then by ID string. That should be deterministic.)\n7. For each node id in that order:\n   - Get node from dict.\n   - If node is not removable (removable == false) or node symbol in required_symbols or node is root (parent None): skip.\n   - If kept_children_count[node.id] > 0: skip because it has retained children.\n   - Now check if removing this node would cause any required symbol count to drop to zero. Since we only remove this node itself (because all children are already removed), we need to check for each required_symbol: current count of that symbol minus 1 if node.symbol == that symbol must be >= 1. If node.symbol is not required, then no change. So we can check: if node.symbol in required_symbols and count_per_symbol[node.symbol] == 1: then removal would remove the last occurrence, so skip.\n   - If passes, then mark node as removed: \n        count_per_symbol[node.symbol] -= 1\n        # update parent's kept_children_count\n        if node.parent is not None:\n            kept_children_count[node.parent] -= 1\n        # also we could add node.id to a removal order list.\n8. After processing all nodes, the set of retained node IDs are those not removed. Also we need to output the actual removal order (list of node IDs in the order they were removed). And sorted retained IDs (sorted? probably by ID string in ascending order? The output spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we return an object with fields \"removed\" (list in removal order) and \"retained\" (sorted list). Also we might need to include other fields? The description says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" But it doesn't specify exactly what fields. Based on common Perses output, we likely need to return the result of the reduction: the list of node IDs that remain. However the instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to output both. The solve function must return a JSON-serializable object. So we can return a dict with \"retained\" and \"removed\". Additionally, the problem states \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So probably the expected output is {\"retained\": [...], \"removed\": [...]}. Let's check the typical problem: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So yes.\n\nAlso we must raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. What constitutes infeasible? The root must exist, required symbols must be present initially? Possibly if a required symbol is not present in any node, that's infeasible. Also if after removal, the required symbols cannot be preserved? But we are always preserving required symbols through the checks. However if initially a required symbol is not present, we should raise ValueError. Also if the tree structure is invalid (e.g., parent references not consistent), we raise ValueError. For simplicity, we assume the input is valid unless a required symbol is missing. We'll validate.\n\nEdge cases: The root is always retained. Required symbols must be represented by at least one node after removal. Since root is always retained and root symbol is usually \"root\", which is in required_symbols, that ensures root is kept. But required_symbols may include other symbols.\n\nWe need to implement `solve(case)` that takes a case dict (the payload) and returns the result. The case has \"payload\" containing \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". We'll process the payload.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Parse payload.\n- Build node dict: map id -> node dict.\n- Validate: all nodes have id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Ensure root has parent null. Ensure no duplicate ids.\n- Build children map: for each node, if parent not None, add child id to parent's children list.\n- Count initial symbol frequencies.\n- Set required_symbols set.\n- Check that all required_symbols are present in at least one node initially. If not, raise ValueError.\n- Initialize kept_children_count: for each node, length of children list.\n- Process nodes in order as described.\n- After loop, collect retained IDs (node ids not removed). Sort them (by natural string order? Probably lexicographic ascending). Collect removed IDs in order of removal.\n- Return {\"retained\": sorted_retained, \"removed\": removed_order}\n\nNote: The interaction between \"removable\" field and depth ordering: we only consider nodes that are removable. Also nodes with symbol in required_symbols cannot be removed, regardless of removable flag? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So required symbol nodes are not removable even if removable flag is true. So we treat them as not eligible.\n\nAlso note: The root is not in required_symbols? Actually \"root\" is a symbol, and it's in required_symbols in the examples. So root symbol is required, so we never remove root. Good.\n\nNow we must ensure we use only Python standard library. Sorting by depth descendant and then by id ascending: we can use `sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))`. But careful: depths are integers, IDs are strings. That's fine.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the removal order is the order in which we removed nodes. Since we iterate over sorted nodes and conditionally remove, the removal order is the iteration order (with skipping). We'll append node ID to a list when we remove.\n\nOne more nuance: The condition \"Never remove a node with retained children\" is checked via kept_children_count. But when we remove a node, we decrease its parent's kept_children_count. So when we later process the parent, its kept_children_count reflects current state.\n\nNow test with the given fixtures. We'll implement and test mentally. For case 000, the nodes: depth 0 root (symbol root, not removable). depth 1: n1 (s1, not removable), n2 (s2, removable), n3 (s0, not removable). depth 2: n4 (s1, removable, parent n2), n5 (s2, not removable, parent n3), n6 (s0, removable, parent n1). required_symbols: root, s1. Process deepest first: depth 2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Sort IDs: n4, n5, n6.\n- n4: removable, symbol s1 is required? s1 is in required_symbols, so skip (required symbol).\n- n5: not removable -> skip.\n- n6: removable, symbol s0 not required. Check kept children: n6 has no children? None (depth 2, not listed as parent for any node). So kept_children_count = 0. Check required symbols: removing n6 would remove its symbol s0, not required, so no problem. Remove n6: decrement count for s0, update parent n1's kept_children_count: n1 had 1 child (n6), now becomes 0. Add n6 to removal order.\nNow depth 1 nodes: n1, n2, n3. Sort IDs: n1, n2, n3.\n- n1: symbol s1 (required) -> skip.\n- n2: removable, symbol s2 not required. Check kept children: n2 originally had one child n4. n4 is still kept (since we skipped it because it was required). So kept_children_count for n2 is 1 (n4 not removed). So condition fails (has retained child) -> skip.\n- n3: not removable (removable=false) -> skip.\ndepth 0: root is skipped (required symbol, parent None). No more nodes. So removed only n6. Retained: all except n6. Sorted retained IDs: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root (root is id \"root\"? Actually root id is \"root\"). So sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"] (lexicographic: \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"). But \"root\" is after \"n5\" because 'r' > 'n'? Actually 'r' is greater than 'n', so \"root\" comes after \"n5\". So sorted retained: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. Removed: [\"n6\"]. This seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify that required symbols remain: s1 is present at n1 and n4; root is present at root. So okay. This matches the typical Perses behavior where we remove deep nodes that are not required and don't affect coverage.\n\nLet's test case 001 quickly. Similar structure. depth 2: n4 (s1 required), n5 (s2 not removable), n6 (s0 removable). Process n4 skip, n5 skip, n6 removable, no children, symbol s0 not required -> remove n6, update n1's kept children to 0. depth 1: n1 skip (s1 required), n2 removable with child n4 (s1 required, kept) -> has retained child -> skip. n3 not removable. So removed n6 only. Retained all others.\n\nCase 002 similar.\n\nThus our algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow we need to output only JSON with key \"implementation\". The implementation must be a Python source string that defines `solve(case)`. We'll write that as a string.\n\nWe must be careful to escape newlines, backslashes, and quotes. We'll write the code as a string and then use json.dumps to produce the JSON. Since we are to return the entire response as a JSON object with key \"implementation\", we'll format the code inside.\n\nLet's write the solve function."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1852,"completion_tokens":6082,"total_tokens":7934,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5447},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":60},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}